# Line endings for anything that executes inside the Linux container or as a
# git hook. CRLF is invisible on Windows and fatal there — bash reads the CR
# as part of the last token on every line, so a script silently takes the
# wrong branch and reports success.
#
# An attribute cannot rescue a file git has already classified as binary. If a
# script misbehaves, run `git ls-files --eol <path>`: `i/-text` means the rule
# was skipped, usually because a raw control byte is embedded in the source.
# Remove the byte first, then `git add --renormalize`.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Markdown is normalized to LF as well as getting the prose diff driver.
# diff=markdown alone only selects a diff driver; line endings stay governed by
# the checkout's core.autocrlf. On a Windows clone that writes every .md as
# CRLF, which the container's git then reports as modified -- phantom changes
# on files nobody touched, and real line-ending churn the moment one is
# committed from inside the container.
#
# The built-in markdown driver puts the enclosing heading in the hunk header
# instead of a random nearby line.
*.md   text eol=lf diff=markdown
*.qmd  text eol=lf diff=markdown
